---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout
  title="Shader Effects — Varve Docs"
  description="Use Varve's curated shader effects through the normal editable effect, mask, history, persistence, and export workflows."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <article class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Tools / Shader Effects
      </nav>

      <h1>Shader Effects</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        Shader Effects are curated procedural and image-processing treatments exposed through
        Effect Studio and Object Filters. They are editable document data, not flattened previews
        and not arbitrary code execution.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Use an effect</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Select a compatible shape, image, text object, or group.</li>
          <li>Open <strong>Effect Studio</strong> for named treatments, or use <strong>Object Filters</strong> for direct stack editing.</li>
          <li>Choose a treatment such as Bloom, RGB Split, CRT, VHS, Palette Snap, Lens Flare, Light Leak, or Caustics.</li>
          <li>Adjust only the controls that describe the look: scale, angle, origin, colors, intensity, seed, and quality. Use Before/After comparison before committing.</li>
          <li>Reorder, mask, blend, duplicate, undo, save, reopen, and export through the same workflows as other effects.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>What is stored</h2>
        <p>
          Documents store the effect kind, artistic parameters, seed/time values where applicable,
          asset references, and algorithm versions needed to preserve meaning. They do not store GPU
          devices, bind groups, compiled pipelines, mapped buffers, or temporary preview URLs.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Backend and fallback</h2>
        <p>
          The interactive canvas keeps the tested Canvas2D/software path authoritative. An async
          consumer may use native or WebGPU compute when capability detection, shader compilation,
          resource validation, and the request all succeed. The software implementation remains
          available for unsupported adapters, Linux WebKitGTK, older browsers, and device loss.
          A successful API probe alone is not evidence that a GPU produced the pixels.
        </p>
        <dl class="state-list">
          <div><dt>Usable acceleration</dt><dd>The selected provider initialized, compiled the requested effect, submitted it, and returned a valid result.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Software fallback</dt><dd>The provider was unavailable or failed validation/runtime checks; the same authored parameters are evaluated by the tested CPU implementation.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Unresolved failure</dt><dd>If no provider can produce a result, export reports the error instead of silently writing the unshaded source.</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Coordinates, color, and export</h2>
        <p>
          Pixel centers, source/document coordinates, effect bounds, and producer dimensions are
          explicit. Procedural phase does not reset when the canvas pans or zooms. Current GPU
          effects use a bounded sRGB RGBA8 surface with straight-alpha boundaries; premultiplied
          values are internal only where a kernel needs them. This is an SDR contract, not an HDR
          or wide-gamut promise.
        </p>
        <p>
          Export evaluates committed parameters at export quality and includes effect expansion
          such as bloom spill. SVG/PDF preserve unaffected vector and text content, rasterizing the
          smallest dependency region when the target format cannot represent a treatment.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Why not arbitrary shader imports?</h2>
        <p>
          A Shadertoy, GLSL, or arbitrary WGSL file cannot be treated as automatically portable or
          safely preemptible GPU work. Varve does not execute user JavaScript, native code, hidden
          network requests, or unrestricted filesystem access for an effect. Any future authoring
          surface must define a supported language subset, stages, entry points, bindings, resource
          budgets, diagnostics, versioned presets, and a last-known-good recovery path first.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="docs-cta">
        <h2>Related guides</h2>
        <p><a href={sitePath('/features/shader-effects')}>Shader Effects overview</a> · <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/effect-studio')}>Effect Studio</a> · <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/halftone')}>Halftone &amp; Screens</a></p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0 5rem; }
  .breadcrumb { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: .875rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a, .docs-section a, .docs-cta a { color: var(--text-link); }
  h1 { color: var(--text-primary); font: 700 clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.8rem)/1.1 var(--font-editorial); margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .docs-intro { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .docs-section { margin-bottom: 2.75rem; }
  .docs-section h2 { border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-accent); color: var(--text-primary); font-size: 1.45rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; padding-bottom: .5rem; }
  .docs-section p, .docs-section li, .state-list dd { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.8; }
  .docs-section li { margin-bottom: .5rem; }
  .docs-section strong, .state-list dt { color: var(--text-primary); }
  .state-list { display: grid; gap: .85rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 0; }
  .state-list div { border-left: 2px solid var(--border-accent); padding-left: 1rem; }
  .state-list dt { font-weight: 700; }
  .state-list dd { margin: .25rem 0 0; }
  .docs-cta { border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default); padding-top: 2.5rem; text-align: center; }
  .docs-cta h2 { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: 1.25rem; }
  .docs-cta p { color: var(--text-secondary); }
</style>
